Help that is asked for goes to stdout and exits 0;
a parse error goes to stderr, followed by the usage, and exits 2.
The usage of flag.CommandLine is silenced while parsing,
so the usage is printed once, after the stream is known.
No variable is declared here, so the main body may declare any name.
@<UI@/Command Line@/Go@/parse command line arguments@=
@<.@/register flags@>flag.Usage = func() {
fmt.Fprint(flag.CommandLine.Output(), @<.@/usage literal@>)
}
flag.CommandLine.Init(os.Args[0], flag.ContinueOnError)
flag.CommandLine.Usage = func() {}
if err := flag.CommandLine.Parse(os.Args[1:]); err == flag.ErrHelp {
flag.CommandLine.SetOutput(os.Stdout)
flag.Usage()
os.Exit(0)
} else if err != nil {
flag.Usage()
os.Exit(2)
}
