@~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/classes@/InvocationSyntax @~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/functions@/all_options @~ Tool@/Python@/functions@/configuration @~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/functions@/metavar @~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/functions@/option_names @
The usage lines list every usage of the command, one per line, after "Usage: ".
@<UI@/Command Line@/command line usage@=
@<Tool@/Text Processing@/basic python settings@>
@<.@/invocation syntax helpers@>


def usage_line(usage):
    words = [syntax.name]
    words += [f'[{option_with_value(opt)}]' for opt in usage.options if not opt.hide]
    words += map(placeholder, usage.arguments)
    return ' '.join(words)


syntax = configuration(InvocationSyntax, r'''
@<.@/invocation syntax@>
''')
print('Usage: ' + '\n       '.join(map(usage_line, syntax.usages)))
@|Tool@/evaluate
@~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/classes@/InvocationSyntax @~ Tool@/Python@/functions@/configuration @~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/functions@/option_with_value @~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/functions@/placeholder @
The invocation syntax of a command is one Jsonnet object.
The usage lines and the manpage of the command are generated from it here,
and a structure turns it into the parser of its language,
so every form of the command line comes from this one chunk.
A usage is one way to call the command: its options and its positional arguments.
A command with one usage may give its options and arguments at the top level instead.
An option is a switch when its type is bool; any other type takes a value,
shown by its argument name, or by its own name, in upper case.
The type is bool, string, int, int64, uint, uint64, or float64,
and a default must suit it. A hidden option is left out of usage lines.
A structure rejects the features its language does not support.

{
    name: COMMAND_NAME,
    usages: [
        {
            name: USAGE_NAME,
            options: [
                {
                    name: LONG_NAME,
                    short: SHORT_NAME (optional),
                    type: TYPE (optional, bool by default),
                    argument: ARGUMENT_NAME (optional),
                    default: DEFAULT (optional),
                    description: DESCRIPTION (optional),
                    hide: HIDDEN_FROM_USAGE (optional),
                },
                ...
            ],
            arguments: [
                ARGUMENT,
                ...
            ],
        },
        ...
    ],
}

@<UI@/Command Line@/invocation syntax@=
{}
@~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/classes@/InvocationSyntax @
The generators include this chunk as one fragment, on top of the shared models,
so an option name becomes the same Go field in each of them.
The Go field is the name in camel case; hyphens and spaces separate words.
@<UI@/Command Line@/Go@/flag syntax helpers@=
@<UI@/Command Line@/invocation syntax helpers@>

GO_KEYWORDS = {
    'break', 'default', 'func', 'interface', 'select',
    'case', 'defer', 'go', 'map', 'struct',
    'chan', 'else', 'goto', 'package', 'switch',
    'const', 'fallthrough', 'if', 'range', 'type',
    'continue', 'for', 'import', 'return', 'var',
}


def field_name(name):
    parts = [part for part in re.split(r'[-_]+', re.sub(r'\s+', '-', name)) if part]
    if not parts:
        return ''
    return parts[0].lower() + ''.join(part.capitalize() for part in parts[1:])


def take_field(name):
    field = field_name(name)
    if not re.fullmatch(r'[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*', field):
        raise ValueError(f"name '{name}' is not a Go field")
    if field in GO_KEYWORDS:
        raise ValueError(f"name '{name}' is a Go keyword")
    return field
@
The structures include this chunk in their generators,
so every generator validates the invocation syntax the same way.
A short name is one letter or digit.
@<UI@/Command Line@/invocation syntax helpers@=
class Option(pydantic.BaseModel):
    model_config = pydantic.ConfigDict(extra='forbid')
    name: str = pydantic.Field(pattern=r'^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$')
    short: str | None = pydantic.Field(None, pattern=r'^[0-9A-Za-z]$')
    type: Literal['bool', 'string', 'int', 'int64', 'uint', 'uint64', 'float64'] = 'bool'
    argument: str | None = None
    default: bool | int | float | str | None = None
    description: str = ''
    hide: bool = False

    @pydantic.model_validator(mode='after')
    def check_default(self):
        value = self.default
        if value is None:
            return self
        if self.type == 'bool':
            suits = isinstance(value, bool)
        elif self.type == 'string':
            suits = isinstance(value, str)
        elif self.type == 'float64':
            suits = isinstance(value, (int, float)) and not isinstance(value, bool)
        else:
            suits = (isinstance(value, int) and not isinstance(value, bool)
                     and (value >= 0 or not self.type.startswith('u')))
        if not suits:
            raise ValueError(f'default {value!r} does not suit type {self.type}')
        return self
@~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/classes@/Option @
Options and arguments at the top level become one usage named after the command.
Usage names, option names, and short names are each unique in the whole command,
because its parser reads the options of every usage.
@<UI@/Command Line@/invocation syntax helpers@+
class Usage(pydantic.BaseModel):
    model_config = pydantic.ConfigDict(extra='forbid')
    name: str
    options: list[Option] = []
    arguments: list[str] = []


class InvocationSyntax(pydantic.BaseModel):
    model_config = pydantic.ConfigDict(extra='forbid')
    name: str = 'command'
    usages: list[Usage] = pydantic.Field(min_length=1)

    @pydantic.model_validator(mode='before')
    @classmethod
    def gather_single_usage(cls, data):
        if isinstance(data, dict) and 'usages' not in data:
            data = dict(data)
            usage = {'name': data.get('name', 'command')}
            for key in ('options', 'arguments'):
                if key in data:
                    usage[key] = data.pop(key)
            data['usages'] = [usage]
        return data

    @pydantic.model_validator(mode='after')
    def check_unique_names(self):
        options = all_options(self)
        for kind, names in (
            ('usage', [usage.name for usage in self.usages]),
            ('option', [opt.name for opt in options]),
            ('short option', [opt.short for opt in options if opt.short]),
        ):
            for name in names:
                if names.count(name) > 1:
                    raise ValueError(f'{kind} {name!r} is given twice')
        return self
@~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/classes@/InvocationSyntax @~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/classes@/Option @~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/classes@/Usage @~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/functions@/all_options @
A placeholder stands for a value in a usage line: upper case, with underscores between words.
@<UI@/Command Line@/invocation syntax helpers@+
def all_options(syntax):
    return [opt for usage in syntax.usages for opt in usage.options]


def placeholder(name):
    return name.upper().replace('-', '_').replace(' ', '_')


def metavar(opt):
    return placeholder(opt.argument or opt.name)
@~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/functions@/all_options @~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/functions@/metavar @~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/functions@/placeholder @
An option is shown by its short name if it has one, otherwise by its long name.
A long name follows two hyphens in the usage lines and the manpage of every structure.
@<UI@/Command Line@/invocation syntax helpers@+
LONG_PREFIX = '--'


def option_name(opt):
    return f'-{opt.short}' if opt.short else LONG_PREFIX + opt.name


def option_with_value(opt):
    return option_name(opt) + ('' if opt.type == 'bool' else f' {metavar(opt)}')
@~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/functions@/metavar @~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/functions@/option_name @~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/functions@/option_with_value @~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/variables@/LONG_PREFIX @
A description of an option names it both ways, the short name first.
@<UI@/Command Line@/invocation syntax helpers@+
def option_names(opt):
    return ([f'-{opt.short}'] if opt.short else []) + [LONG_PREFIX + opt.name]
@~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/functions@/option_names @~ UI@/Command Line@/Python@/variables@/LONG_PREFIX @
@<UI@/Command Line@/Go@/flag syntax helpers@+
FLAG_FUNCS = {
    'bool': 'BoolVar',
    'string': 'StringVar',
    'int': 'IntVar',
    'int64': 'Int64Var',
    'uint': 'UintVar',
    'uint64': 'Uint64Var',
    'float64': 'Float64Var',
}


def go_string(text):
    for raw, escaped in (('\\', '\\\\'), ('"', '\\"'), ('\n', '\\n'), ('\r', '\\r'), ('\t', '\\t')):
        text = text.replace(raw, escaped)
    return f'"{text}"'


def go_literal(opt):
    if opt.default is None:
        return {'bool': 'false', 'string': '""'}.get(opt.type, '0')
    if opt.type == 'bool':
        return 'true' if opt.default else 'false'
    if opt.type == 'string':
        return go_string(opt.default)
    return str(opt.default)
@
The flag package parses one usage, so a command has one usage; its positional arguments are an exact list.
A short name is a second flag, so no name may be both a short and a long name,
and h and help stay the help flags of the flag package.
Options, and separately arguments, must become distinct Go fields.
@<UI@/Command Line@/Go@/flag syntax helpers@+
class GoSyntax(InvocationSyntax):
    @pydantic.model_validator(mode='after')
    def check_go_usage(self):
        if len(self.usages) != 1:
            raise ValueError('the flag package parses one usage')
        usage = self.usages[0]
        flags = [opt.name for opt in usage.options] + [opt.short for opt in usage.options if opt.short]
        for name in flags:
            if name in ('h', 'help'):
                raise ValueError(f'flag {name!r} is the help flag of the flag package')
            if flags.count(name) > 1:
                raise ValueError(f'flag {name!r} is both a short name and a long name')
        for names in ([opt.name for opt in usage.options], usage.arguments):
            seen = set()
            for name in names:
                field = take_field(name)
                if field in seen:
                    raise ValueError(f"name '{name}' repeats field {field}")
                seen.add(field)
        return self
@
command is the rightmost label of a package main program.
These blocks set the package name to main,
add the flag, fmt, and os imports,
add the command variable when the invocation syntax needs one,
and add func main.
func main parses the command line and then runs the main body.
Parsing always uses the flag, fmt, and os packages,
so those three imports stay used when the command has no flags.
Replace module meta information to name the other files in the node.
That replacement does not rename the command.
@<command@:Language@/Go@/File@/Meta@/package name@=main@
@<Chunk@/Language@/Go@/Module@=
{"description": "Structures of a whole Go module"}
@

@<Language@/Go@/Module@/Go Command@/build packages@=#!/bin/sh
@<.@/go command settings@>
go build ./...
echo 'go build ./...: ok'
@

