@|Tool@/evaluate@
A text processing script is a Python script that declares its dependencies:
jsonnet evaluates configuration, and pydantic validates it.
Its interpreter runs it in a background process that has loaded them already.
@<Tool@/Text Processing@/basic python settings@=
#!@<.@/Runtime@/path to python interpreter@>
@<.@/Runtime@/python dependencies@>
import os
import re
import sys
from typing import Literal

@<.@/Runtime@/import dependencies@>

@<.@/Runtime@/configuration helpers@>
@
The interpreter of a script is the client of a background Python process.
The client and the server are written to a directory named by their checksum,
private to the user, so a changed client, server, or python3 gets its own server.
The directory is moved into place whole, so a concurrent evaluation never sees it half written.
It lies in the filter cache of litar when there is one, because the cache keeps the output
of this chunk, the path of the client, and both must go away together.
@<Tool@/Text Processing@/Runtime@/path to python interpreter@=
#!/bin/sh
set -e
if [ -n "$LITAR_CACHE" ]; then
    base=$LITAR_CACHE/python
else
    base=${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-${TMPDIR:-/tmp}}/litar-python-`id -u`
fi
mkdir -p -m 700 "$base"
if [ ! -O "$base" ]; then
    echo "$base is not owned by the user" >&2
    exit 1
fi
temp=`mktemp -d "$base/new.XXXXXX"`
trap 'rm -rf "$temp"' EXIT
printf '#!%s -S\n' "`which python3`" > "$temp/client"
cat >> "$temp/client" <<'LITAR_PYTHON_CLIENT'
@<.@/python client@>
LITAR_PYTHON_CLIENT
cat > "$temp/server" <<'LITAR_PYTHON_SERVER'
@<.@/python server@>
LITAR_PYTHON_SERVER
chmod +x "$temp/client"
dir=$base/`cat "$temp/client" "$temp/server" | cksum | tr ' ' -`
if [ ! -x "$dir/client" ]; then
    mv -T "$temp" "$dir" 2>/dev/null || [ -x "$dir/client" ]
fi
echo "$dir/client"
@|Tool@/evaluate@
The client is the interpreter of a script.
It passes its standard streams, arguments, directory and environment to the server,
then exits with the status the server reports.
When no server listens, it starts one in the background and runs this script itself,
so only the first script pays for loading the dependencies.
The server leaves the working directory, a view that is unmounted after the script,
and closes the standard streams, which the evaluation reads until they close.
@<Tool@/Text Processing@/Runtime@/python client@=
import json
import os
import socket
import sys

here = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))


def start_server():
    if os.fork() == 0:
        os.setsid()
        os.chdir('/')
        null = os.open(os.devnull, os.O_RDWR)
        for fd in range(3):
            os.dup2(null, fd)
        server = os.path.join(here, 'server')
        os.execv(sys.executable, [sys.executable, server])


try:
    connection = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
    connection.connect(os.path.join(here, 'socket'))
except OSError:
    start_server()
    os.execv(sys.executable, [sys.executable, *sys.argv[1:]])
request = {
    'argv': [os.path.abspath(sys.argv[1]), *sys.argv[2:]],
    'cwd': os.getcwd(),
    'env': dict(os.environ),
}
socket.send_fds(connection, [b'\0'], [0, 1, 2])
connection.sendall(json.dumps(request).encode())
connection.shutdown(socket.SHUT_WR)
status = b''
while chunk := connection.recv(64):
    status += chunk
sys.exit(int(status) if status else 1)
@
The server loads the dependencies once, then forks a fresh child for every connection,
so each script starts from the same state and none sees what another changed.
A lock keeps one server per directory; a second one exits at once.
After ten idle minutes the server removes its socket, so clients start a new server,
serves the connections already waiting, and exits.
@<Tool@/Text Processing@/Runtime@/python server@=
@<.@/python dependencies@>
import fcntl
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import runpy
import signal
import socket
import sys
import traceback
from typing import Literal

@<.@/import dependencies@>

@<.@/preload pydantic@>

here = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))

@<.@/cache jsonnet@>

@<.@/run a script@>

@<.@/serve a connection@>


def accept_all():
    while True:
        try:
            connection, _ = listener.accept()
        except (TimeoutError, BlockingIOError):
            return
        if os.fork() == 0:
            serve(connection)
        connection.close()


lock = open(os.path.join(here, 'lock'), 'w')
try:
    fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
except OSError:
    sys.exit()
path = os.path.join(here, 'socket')
if os.path.lexists(path):
    os.unlink(path)
listener = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
listener.bind(path)
listener.listen(64)
listener.settimeout(600)
signal.signal(signal.SIGCHLD, signal.SIG_IGN)
accept_all()
os.unlink(path)
listener.setblocking(False)
accept_all()
@
The inline script metadata tells uv what a script, or the background process, depends on.
@<Tool@/Text Processing@/Runtime@/python dependencies@=
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.10"
# dependencies = ["jsonnet>=0.20", "pydantic>=2"]
# ///
@
When python3 cannot import the dependencies, the script runs itself again through uv,
which provides them from its cache, downloading them on the first run.
The marker variable stops a second attempt, and is removed so nested scripts start afresh.
In the background process the imports already succeeded, so a script never reaches uv there.
@<Tool@/Text Processing@/Runtime@/import dependencies@=
try:
    import _jsonnet
    import pydantic
except ImportError:
    if os.environ.pop('LITAR_UV_RUN', None):
        raise
    os.environ['LITAR_UV_RUN'] = '1'
    os.execvp('uv', ['uv', 'run', '--quiet', '--script', *sys.argv])
os.environ.pop('LITAR_UV_RUN', None)
@
The child of a connection takes the client's standard streams, arguments, directory
and environment, restores the default handling of exited children for the script,
and reports its status. When the child fails before that, the client exits with 1.
@<Tool@/Text Processing@/Runtime@/serve a connection@=
def serve(connection):
    try:
        connection.setblocking(True)
        signal.signal(signal.SIGCHLD, signal.SIG_DFL)
        lock.close()
        listener.close()
        _, fds, _, _ = socket.recv_fds(connection, 1, 3)
        data = b''
        while chunk := connection.recv(65536):
            data += chunk
        request = json.loads(data)
        for target, fd in enumerate(fds):
            os.dup2(fd, target)
            os.close(fd)
        os.chdir(request['cwd'])
        os.environ.clear()
        os.environ.update(request['env'])
        sys.argv = request['argv']
        connection.sendall(str(run(sys.argv[0])).encode())
    except BaseException:
        traceback.print_exc()
    os._exit(0)
@
A script ends as it would in its own interpreter:
sys.exit with a message prints it and gives 1, an uncaught exception prints its traceback,
and the output is flushed before the status is known.
@<Tool@/Text Processing@/Runtime@/run a script@=
def run(script):
    try:
        runpy.run_path(script, run_name='__main__')
        status = 0
    except SystemExit as error:
        status = error.code
        if status is None:
            status = 0
        elif not isinstance(status, int):
            print(status, file=sys.stderr)
            status = 1
    except BaseException:
        traceback.print_exc()
        status = 1
    for stream in sys.stdout, sys.stderr:
        try:
            stream.flush()
        except OSError:
            status = status or 120
    return status
@
pydantic imports its parts on first use and builds its validators on the first model,
so the server does both once, before forking, instead of every script doing them.
@<Tool@/Text Processing@/Runtime@/preload pydantic@=
class Preload(pydantic.BaseModel):
    model_config = pydantic.ConfigDict(extra='allow')
    name: str = pydantic.Field('', pattern=r'^$')


Preload.model_validate_json('{}')
@
jsonnet parses its standard library again on every evaluation, which costs more than most scripts.
The server replaces evaluate_snippet, so its scripts keep each result in a file
named by the hash of the source, shared by later scripts and later builds.
A source that may import a file, or an evaluation with options, is never cached,
because its result depends on more than the source; a failed evaluation is not cached either.
The result is written aside and renamed, so a concurrent script never reads it half written.
@<Tool@/Text Processing@/Runtime@/cache jsonnet@=
def cached(evaluate):
    cache = os.path.join(here, 'jsonnet')
    os.makedirs(cache, exist_ok=True)

    def evaluate_snippet(filename, source, **options):
        if options or 'import' in source:
            return evaluate(filename, source, **options)
        key = hashlib.sha256(f'{filename}\0{source}'.encode()).hexdigest()
        path = os.path.join(cache, key)
        try:
            with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as file:
                return file.read()
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass
        value = evaluate(filename, source)
        temp = f'{path}.{os.getpid()}'
        with open(temp, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as file:
            file.write(value)
        os.replace(temp, path)
        return value

    return evaluate_snippet


_jsonnet.evaluate_snippet = cached(_jsonnet.evaluate_snippet)
@
Configuration is a Jsonnet expression, so a block can extend an object of a base node with +.
A pydantic model validates the evaluated value.
An invalid configuration, like any fail, prints a message and fails the evaluation.
@<Tool@/Text Processing@/Runtime@/configuration helpers@=
def fail(message):
    sys.exit(message)


def configuration(model, source):
    try:
        value = _jsonnet.evaluate_snippet('configuration', source)
    except RuntimeError as error:
        fail(str(error))
    try:
        return model.model_validate_json(value)
    except pydantic.ValidationError as error:
        fail(str(error))
@~ Tool@/Python@/functions@/configuration @~ Tool@/Python@/functions@/fail @
A module path has no blank and does not start with a slash;
a Go version is 1.N or 1.N.P.
Further fields are kept for other generators.
@<Language@/Go@/Module@/Meta@/project meta model@=
class ProjectMeta(pydantic.BaseModel):
    model_config = pydantic.ConfigDict(extra='allow')
    module: str = pydantic.Field(pattern=r'^[^\s/]\S*$')
    go: str = pydantic.Field(pattern=r'^1\.\d+(\.\d+)?$')
@
A Go project is one Go module: a go.mod file at the root of the module,
and packages in directories below it.
One node holds one project, so the blocks of this module carry no label,
and every file of the node sees the same module.
A package is a label, as in the Go file structure,
and each file of a package carries that label with a label of its own.
A command is a package whose rightmost label is command;
by convention its files live in cmd, in a directory named after the command.
A test file is one more file of its package, named after the file it tests with _test,
and it adds testing to its own import paths.
Declare go.mod and every Go file in the file set go module,
so the project checks below see the whole project.

The module field is the module path, and the go field is the minimum Go version.
example.com/project stands in until a project sets its own path.
A project sets its fields in project properties, the fields of a second Jsonnet object,
which override these defaults; Jsonnet rejects a field given twice.
@<Language@/Go@/Module@/Meta@/project meta information@=
{
    module: 'example.com/project',
    go: '1.24',
} + {
    @<..@/project properties@>
}
@
@<Chunk@/Language@/Go@/Module@/Meta@/project meta information@=
{"description": "Module path, Go version, and other properties, as a Jsonnet object."}
@
@<Chunk@/Language@/Go@/Module@/project properties@=
{"description": "Jsonnet fields that override the project meta information."}
@
@<Chunk@/Language@/Go@/Module@/Meta@/project meta model@=
{"description": "Pydantic model that validates the project meta information."}
@

The commands are built in a view of the file set go module, like the checks.
The view must also hold the command directory; a composition wrapping the project
maps it to a directory of the host, so the binaries leave the view.
Each binary is named after the directory of its command;
the go command replaces an existing binary instead of writing into it.
@<Language@/Go@/Module@/Go Command@/build commands@=#!/bin/sh
@<.@/go command settings@>
go build -o '@<..@/command directory@>/' ./cmd/...
echo 'go build -o @<..@/command directory@>/ ./cmd/...: ok'
@

The command directory is bin, below the module root of the view.
@<Language@/Go@/Module@/command directory@=bin@


@<Chunk@/Language@/Go@/Module@/Go Command@/build commands@=
{"description": "Build every command of the project into the command directory."}
@

@<Chunk@/Language@/Go@/Module@/command directory@=
{"description": "Directory of the view, below the module root, that receives the built commands."}
@

The go command reports a failed test on stdout, and the output of a failed filter is dropped,
so the report is kept and written to stderr when a test fails.
Declare every test file in the file set go tests as well as in go module,
so a view that only needs the program can subtract the tests.
@<Language@/Go@/Module@/Go Command@/test packages@=#!/bin/sh
@<.@/go command settings@>
report=$(go test ./... 2>&1) || {
	printf '%s\n' "$report" >&2
	exit 1
}
printf '%s\n' "$report"
@

Each check runs the go command in a view of the file set go module,
so the module root is the working directory.
GOTOOLCHAIN=local keeps the go command from downloading another toolchain,
and GOWORK=off ignores a workspace file outside the view.
The build cache and the module cache stay at their host locations.
A failed check fails the evaluation, and the go command reports on stderr.
@<Language@/Go@/Module@/Go Command@/go command settings@=
set -e
export GOTOOLCHAIN=local
export GOWORK=off
@
@<Chunk@/Language@=
{"description": "Common structures found in languages."}
@
@<Chunk@/Language@/C@=
{"description": "Structures of C programming."}
@
@<Chunk@/Language@/Go@=
{"description": "Structures of Go programming."}
@
@<Chunk@/UI@=
{"description": "Shared schemas and generators of user interfaces."}
@
@<Chunk@/UI@/Command Line@/C@=
{"description": "Parse the command line of a C program with getopt, from the invocation syntax."}
@
@<Chunk@/Framework@=
{"description": "Frameworks an application chooses to adopt."}
@
@<Chunk@/Project@=
{"description": "Concrete projects."}
@
@<Index@/UI@/Command Line@/Python@=
{"description": "Top-level names of the Python scripts that read the invocation syntax."}
@
@<Index@/UI@/Command Line@/Python@/functions@=
{"description": "Top-level functions."}
@
One case of an acceptance test is a chunk: its content is a script of checks, and this filter
turns the script into the result of the case, so evaluating the chunk runs the test.
The view of the filter holds the command and the fixtures of the case.
A case that fails still evaluates: its result says what failed, and the report decides.
@<UI@/Command Line@/Test@/run case@=#!/bin/bash
set -u
command=$PWD/@<.@/command@>
@<.@/environment@>
@<.@/Runner@/state of the case@>
@<.@/Runner@/result@>
@<.@/checks@>
@<.@/Runner@/run the script on stdin@>
finish
@
The project names its command with a labelled block, and passes the labels to the filter:
the labels reach every reference of the filter.
@<Chunk@/UI@/Command Line@/Test@/command@=
{"description": "Path of the command under test in the view; set it under the label of the command."}
@
@<Chunk@/UI@/Command Line@/Test@/environment@=
{"description": "Shell lines that prepare the environment of the command under test; set them under the label of the command."}
@
@<Chunk@/UI@/Command Line@/Test@=
{"description": "Acceptance tests of a command: a case is a chunk filtered into its result."}
@
@<Chunk@/UI@/Command Line@/Test@/run case@=
{"description": "Filter that runs the script of one case and prints its result: ok NAME COUNT, skip NAME: REASON, or FAIL NAME with the failed checks."}
@
@<Chunk@/UI@/Command Line@/Test@/summarize results@=
{"description": "Filter that counts the results of the cases, and fails when a case failed or none passed."}
@
@<Chunk@/UI@/Command Line@/Test@/checks@=
{"description": "Shell functions a case script calls; a project may add its own."}
@
The work directory is outside the view, because the view only shows files of the composition.
@<UI@/Command Line@/Test@/Runner@/state of the case@=
name=unnamed
checks=0
failed=
skipped=
work=$(mktemp -d)
trap 'rm -rf "$work"' EXIT
@
The result has no final line break, so a report can list one case on each line.
A case without a check fails: a script that ended early must not look like a success.
@<UI@/Command Line@/Test@/Runner@/result@=
# finish: print the result of the case and end the filter.
finish() {
	if [ -n "$skipped" ]; then
		printf 'skip %s: %s' "$name" "$skipped"
	elif [ -n "$failed" ]; then
		printf 'FAIL %s\n%s' "$name" "${failed%$'\n'}"
	elif [ "$checks" -eq 0 ]; then
		printf 'FAIL %s\n  no check ran' "$name"
	else
		printf 'ok %s %s' "$name" "$checks"
	fi
	exit 0
}

# miss CHECK WHAT [FILE]: record a failed check, with the file that explains it.
miss() {
	failed+="  $1: $2"$'\n'
	if [ $# -gt 2 ] && [ -s "$3" ]; then
		failed+=$(sed 's/^/    /' "$3")$'\n'
	fi
}
@~ UI@/Command Line@/Bash@/functions@/finish @~ UI@/Command Line@/Bash@/functions@/miss @
The script is checked before it runs: a syntax error would otherwise stop it halfway,
after checks that passed.
@<UI@/Command Line@/Test@/Runner@/run the script on stdin@=
cat >"$work/case"
if bash -n "$work/case" 2>"$work/syntax"; then
	. "$work/case"
else
	miss script "syntax error" "$work/syntax"
fi
@~ UI@/Command Line@/Bash@/functions@/miss @
@<UI@/Command Line@/Test@/checks@+
# test_case NAME [DIRECTORY]: name the case, and enter the directory of its fixtures.
test_case() {
	name=$1
	if [ $# -gt 1 ] && ! cd "$2" 2>/dev/null; then
		miss fixtures "no directory $2"
		finish
	fi
}

# skip REASON: end the case without a verdict.
skip() {
	skipped=$1
	finish
}
@~ UI@/Command Line@/Bash@/functions@/finish @~ UI@/Command Line@/Bash@/functions@/miss @
@<UI@/Command Line@/Test@/checks@+
# run ARGUMENT...: run the command; its output goes to $work/out and $work/err,
# its exit status to $status.
run() {
	checks=$((checks + 1))
	"$command" "$@@" >"$work/out" 2>"$work/err"
	status=$?
}
@~ UI@/Command Line@/Bash@/functions@/run @
A passing command must leave stderr empty.
@<UI@/Command Line@/Test@/checks@+
# prints_file CHECK FILE ARGUMENT...: the command succeeds and prints exactly FILE.
prints_file() {
	local check=$1 want=$2
	shift 2
	run "$@@"
	if [ "$status" -ne 0 ]; then
		miss "$check" "exit $status" "$work/err"
	elif [ -s "$work/err" ]; then
		miss "$check" "unexpected stderr" "$work/err"
	elif ! diff -u --label expected --label printed "$want" "$work/out" >"$work/diff"; then
		miss "$check" stdout "$work/diff"
	fi
}

# prints CHECK TEXT ARGUMENT...: the command succeeds and prints exactly TEXT.
prints() {
	local check=$1
	printf '%s' "$2" >"$work/want"
	shift 2
	prints_file "$check" "$work/want" "$@@"
}
@~ UI@/Command Line@/Bash@/functions@/miss @~ UI@/Command Line@/Bash@/functions@/run @
A failing command must leave stdout empty, and its stderr only has to contain the needle.
@<UI@/Command Line@/Test@/checks@+
# fails CHECK NEEDLE ARGUMENT...: the command fails and reports NEEDLE.
fails() {
	local check=$1 needle=$2
	shift 2
	run "$@@"
	if [ "$status" -eq 0 ]; then
		miss "$check" "expected failure"
	elif ! grep -F -q -e "$needle" "$work/err"; then
		miss "$check" "stderr misses [$needle]" "$work/err"
	elif [ -s "$work/out" ]; then
		miss "$check" "stdout not empty" "$work/out"
	fi
}
@~ UI@/Command Line@/Bash@/functions@/miss @~ UI@/Command Line@/Bash@/functions@/run @
The output of a failed filter is dropped, so a failing report goes to stderr.
A report in which no case passed fails too: the cases may all have been lost.
@<UI@/Command Line@/Test@/summarize results@=#!/bin/sh
results=$(cat)
count() {
	printf '%s\n' "$results" | grep -c "^$1 "
}
report=$(printf '%s\npassed %s failed %s skipped %s' "$results" "$(count ok)" "$(count FAIL)" "$(count skip)")
if [ "$(count FAIL)" -ne 0 ] || [ "$(count ok)" -eq 0 ]; then
	printf '%s\n' "$report" >&2
	exit 1
fi
printf '%s\n' "$report"
@
@<Index@/UI@/Command Line@/Bash@/functions@=
{"description": "Shell functions of the acceptance test runner."}
@
